      "id": 1,
      "text": "Old Hill was part of the Paugussett Nation territory before European settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/old_hill_connecticut"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Taylor family founded Old Hill in the late 17th century, giving it the nickname Taylortown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/old_hill_connecticut"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Mid‑19th‑century onion farms in Old Hill supplied Union troops, while its roads aided the Underground Railroad to Weston.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/old_hill_connecticut"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The neighborhood now showcases Colonial Revival and Victorian homes on sloping terrain, drawing professionals and artists.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/old_hill_connecticut"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Old Hill’s elevated position offers scenic valley views that attracted creative and business elites as Westport expanded.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/old_hill_connecticut"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "A forested suburb west of the Saugatuck River, Old Hill hides large residences amid rolling hills and dense woods.",
